<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Principality of Wales</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century–1542</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> was the realm of the native princes of Gwynedd, who from the early 13th century claimed overlordship over the other Welsh rulers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-the-great">Llywelyn the Great</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a>, recognised as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-montgomery">Treaty of Montgomery</a> in 1267, united most of native Wales. After Edward I's conquest of 1282–1283 it became a crown territory, later held by the English heir.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/principality-of-wales.jpg" alt="Principality of Wales" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A455bcd9 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wales_after_the_Treaty_of_Montgomery_1267_Colorblind.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
